member.summary:
The knowledge base on the Compatibility pages (through ) and in the diagnosis reports (through
): known mods relevant for the mod, their pitfalls and advice, and the Harmony co-owners of the
methods the mod patches, by name. On the framework's own page: every active known mod.

The context of the mod whose  subclass is , or null before RimWorld created
that mod class (static constructors of [StaticConstructorOnStartup] classes, defs and patches all run later, so from there it
is safe; inside Register use r.Kit). Handy for patches: var k = Framework.Of<LOMod>(); if (k?.On("walls") != true) return;

An area the mod draws in the card body (above the presets).  gets a rect at the part's top with the body
width (its height is what the part returned last frame) and returns the height it used. Runs inside the card's error fence;
use  and . Several calls add several parts.

Base of a dependent mod's  class. RimWorld finds the subclass by Type; its name decides the settings file
name (Mod_<folder>_<TypeName>.xml), so an existing mod keeps its old class name.

public sealed class LOMod : KitModBase
{
    public LOMod(ModContentPack c) : base(c, new KitInfo { Name = "Luciferium Overhaul", ShortId = "LO", Version = "2.0.0", PerSave = true }) { }
    protected override void Register(KitRegistry r) { r.Section("healing", "Healing"); r.Float("healing", "lo.regrowDays", 10f, 0.1f, 120f, "Days to regrow a limb").InGroup("Regrowth"); }
}

The base constructor does everything in this order: context, log header, API-level check, , settings file
(with legacy migration), the mod's Harmony patch classes one by one.  is already set while Register runs, but
fields of the subclass (and its constructor body) are not: Register runs inside the base constructor.
Every hook below runs inside the mod's error fence; an exception never reaches the game or another mod.

One value of an enum, shown as a dropdown. Labels: <KeyPrefix>.Enum.<EnumTypeName>.<ValueName>, fallback the
value name split at capitals. Stored as the number: never renumber the enum. Read with ;
in a preset use p.Set(key, (float)(int)MyEnum.X).

Window sizing for every window of a mod. Windows declare the size they were designed for at a 900 px tall screen;
 scales it up for larger screens and by the player's global "Window size" preference (Mod settings,
JB Framework), and never lets it leave the screen. Usage: public override Vector2 InitialSize => KitUI.Size(900f, 640f);
